.nh
.TH "RAIL0" "1" "Jul 2026" "rail0 CLI" ""

.SH NAME
rail0-payments-sign - Deposit the payer signature on an already-created payment


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrail0 payments sign  [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Sign an existing unsigned payment with the payer's key.

.PP
\&'payments create' creates AND signs in one step. This command is the recovery
path for when that second half didn't happen: the payment exists at status
\&'unsigned' but carries no signature, because the local signing or the submit
failed (a lost connection, a rate limit, the wrong key). Without it the payment
would be stranded — the only way forward would be creating a duplicate.

.PP
The payer signs locally: the key never leaves this machine, only the signature
is sent. Requires the payer's own key, since the gateway checks that the
recovered signer is the payment's payer.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for sign

.PP
\fB-p\fP, \fB--private-key\fP=""
	Payer's secp256k1 private key (0x-prefixed hex); signs locally, the key never leaves this machine


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--base-url\fP=""
	RAIL0 gateway base URL (env: RAIL0_BASE_URL)

.PP
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Print HTTP request/response details to stderr

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Force plain (uncolored) JSON output (useful in scripts)

.PP
\fB--pretty\fP[=false]
	Use the key-value/table view instead of JSON (terminal only)

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	Print only the result's id/hash, one per line (useful in scripts)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  rail0 payments sign 0xabc123… -p 0xPayerKey…
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrail0-payments(1)\fP
